text:
jamie: Anyone else seeing the Go SSE client buffer balloon when we're streaming large partial JSON payloads? decode errors + goroutine growth under bursty load.

eli: yep seen that. Usually it's the consumer slow path — processing blocks and incoming TCP buffers fill. Are you using json.Decoder.Decode on a continuous stream?

jamie: yep, something like that. It's an SSE stream where each 'data:' contains a partial JSON object (fragments). We try to reassemble then json.Unmarshal. still see spikes.

sara: two quick ideas: 1) bounded worker queue between read loop and processor so you apply backpressure locally, 2) make the read loop drop/skip very old partial frames if queue saturated (best-effort) — depends on correctness guarantees.

lee: +1. Also watch for using json.Decoder.Decode — it expects a complete value and will block until it sees a full object. For framed SSE with split utf8 you want to collect 'data' lines and only unmarshal once you have a full fragment.

eli: example pattern that helped us:

``
scanner := bufio.NewScanner(conn)
scanner.Buffer(make([]byte, 64*1024), 10*1024*1024)
dataC := make(chan []byte, 256) // bounded
// reader
go func(){
  for scanner.Scan() {
    line := scanner.Bytes()
    if strings.HasPrefix(string(line), "data:") {
      b := append([]byte(nil), line[5:]...)
      select {
      case dataC <- b:
      default:
        // backpressure: drop / count metric
      }
    }
  }
  close(dataC)
}()
// processors
for i:=0;i<8;i++{ go worker(dataC) }
```

lee: + Also ensure you don't keep unbounded bytes.Buffer growth if fragments are large. Reuse byte slices from a sync.Pool.

jamie: good call. Is dropping acceptable? for our use-case we can't lose latest partial — but we can drop stale cached prefixes. maybe evict-oldest instead of new.

sara: evict-oldest works if you only care about latest model token stream. If you need exact stream, then you must widen capacity or offload processing to faster service. Another trick: process lightweight metadata inline and offload heavy steps to batch worker.

jamie: ran a quick patch that makes the queue bounded and evicts oldest when full, plus adds a metric 'sse.queue.evicted'. pushed in PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/repo/pull/3124 (small patch)

build-bot: CI passed for PR 3124 :white_check_mark:

ops-bot: scheduled pairing session with @jamie and @eli tomorrow 10:30am PT to watch live load test :calendar:

lee: FYI, also saw issues with split UTF-8 sequences. If you cut mid-rune, json.Unmarshal fails. Use bytes.Runes or keep a tail buffer between scans to complete the rune.

jamie: thanks all. After merge we'll run a 1h soak at 10k concurrent clients and report metrics. If anyone has a simple load generator for SSE partial-json, ping me.

eli: I have a small go harness — I'll DM you link :+1:

jamie: follow-up 6h later: deployed bounded-queue evict-oldest. queue evictions peaked at ~5000 during the spike, error rate dropped from 7% -> 1.8%. still investigating why a few goroutines leaked.

sara: nice improvement. for the goroutine leak add a ctx cancellation in reader and processors; ensure you close channels and waitgroups.

jamie: noted, will add ctx cancel and post another update after the next run.
